{"id": "cores-load-map-8", "at": "2026-11-14T07:50:42+00:00", "kind": "finding", "stage": 1, "lane": "code-review", "commit": "c84a5ab", "text": "cores annotate's Copy list includes up to 80 characters of each named part's text (patient text on a real capture) and says it is for pasting to Claude (annotate_source.py:48,115,133); the samples also stay in browser storage. Nothing tracked, nothing leaves the PC unless pasted. Until fixed: do not paste an annotate Copy list made from a real capture. Fix proposed for stage 2: drop the sample."}
{"id": "cores-load-map-9", "at": "2026-11-14T07:56:03+00:00", "kind": "finding", "stage": 1, "lane": "run-log", "commit": "310639c", "text": "The automatic log folder follows each PC's own OneDrive, work account first (as decided). So a PC on a work OneDrive and a PC on a personal OneDrive get two separate folders whose logs never meet until both are given the same one with cores config --set log_dir FOLDER; and on a work PC the log (computer name, commands, timings, counts; no patient data) sits in the employer's OneDrive. cores log --where and cores doctor show which folder a PC uses; README 'The log' says it. No code change."}
{"id": "cores-load-map-10", "at": "2026-11-14T07:56:11+00:00", "kind": "finding", "stage": 1, "lane": "run-log", "commit": "83184e7", "text": "tests/ will ship in release zips: release.py uses git archive and .gitattributes only leaves out release.py and itself, so cores update would install tests/test_cores_log.py. Harmless. Fix: add 'tests/ export-ignore' to .gitattributes (outside this lane's files), at integration."}
{"id": "cores-load-map-11", "at": "2026-11-14T08:39:38+00:00", "kind": "finding", "stage": 1, "lane": "load-trace", "commit": "8852d80", "text": "Before the Trace is run in CORES: (1) armed, window.global_constant_row_info exists as undefined before CORES creates it (the Trace's getter/setter is the property); typeof and plain reads are unchanged but an 'x in window' test would differ. If CORES behaves differently with the Trace armed, use traceCores({poll: true}) in the Console (touches nothing of the records, no call stacks). (2) Privacy, residual: a clicked button/menu item/link outside the patient list shows its text unless Map's redaction finds an identifier or it has the LAST, FIRST shape; read the Trace result in the HUD before pasting it. (3) Requests through Cerner's own channel (window.external, XMLCclRequest) do not show; the write they lead to still does. All in CORES_PAGE_NOTES.md, section Trace."}
{"id": "cores-load-map-12", "at": "2026-11-14T08:39:43+00:00", "kind": "finding", "stage": 1, "lane": "load-trace", "commit": "34cb531", "text": "For integration: the HUD self-test is 'node test/run_selftest.mjs' (Node 22+, Python, Edge/Chrome over the DevTools protocol; Edge's --dump-dom prints nothing here) and the fake page is served by 'python test/serve.py' (test/ and capture_report.js only, 127.0.0.1), not python -m http.server, which would serve reports\\ too. 'node test/run_selftest.mjs --capture FILE' writes a fake capture (invented patients, with the CORES log comment) for the end-to-end check. test/ ships in release zips like tests/ unless .gitattributes export-ignores both. README.md does not mention the Trace or test/. CORES log contract as built: no comment when there are no events or with log:false; a capture carries events up to its own delivery (its copied/downloaded/verdict arrive with the next capture); vocabulary = LOG_EV in capture_report.js, tabled in CORES_PAGE_NOTES.md."}
