{"id": "cores-load-map-20", "at": "2026-11-14T13:23:09+00:00", "kind": "finding", "stage": 2, "lane": "review-picks", "commit": "d519aad", "text": "cores capture --auto --trace ends the paste with captureCores({auto: true}); then traceCores(); (as planned), so the Trace is armed only after the capture call returns. If capture_report.js presses Reports inside that call (not from a timer afterwards), the Trace misses the Reports click that fills the records. For auto-open and integration to check; no change on the PC side."}
{"id": "cores-load-map-21", "at": "2026-11-14T13:31:15+00:00", "kind": "finding", "stage": 2, "lane": "review-picks", "commit": "608cb05", "text": "cores annotate (finding 1): fixed in c2b7c05, with two things the review did not say. (1) The parser's name could carry patient text too (a label read from free text, e.g. a first name before a colon): now a piece read from the page is copied only when 3 or more patients' rows have it. (2) Annotate copies made before the fix keep the old script: do not paste the Copy list of an old '... - annotate.html'; run cores annotate again or cores clean it."}
{"id": "cores-load-map-22", "at": "2026-11-14T13:31:17+00:00", "kind": "finding", "stage": 2, "lane": "review-picks", "commit": "9eb226b", "text": "Clipboard (finding 2): fixed in c2b7c05 for the clipboard itself. Windows clipboard history (Win+V), when on, keeps its own copy of the report and cores cannot clear it without wiping everything else copied, so it does not. Off on this PC; cores doctor now says whether it is on. Worth one look on the work PC."}
{"id": "cores-load-map-23", "at": "2026-11-14T13:40:38+00:00", "kind": "finding", "stage": 2, "lane": "review-picks", "commit": "0868ecc", "text": "M3 (e69ab2d) choices David may want to change: (1) cores generate warns when the capture is more than 6 hours old (the review said 'a few hours'). (2) A capture's age comes from the time in its name, i.e. the CORES session's clock; if that clock and the PC's are in different time zones the age shown and the warning are off by the difference (the Downloads-or-clipboard choice is not affected). (3) A row that cannot be read keeps the bed and name only, on purpose. (4) Plain cores capture pastes exactly what it did; one printed line changed wording ('newest capture (Downloads or the clipboard)')."}
{"id": "cores-load-map-24", "at": "2026-11-14T13:55:51+00:00", "kind": "finding", "stage": 2, "lane": "auto-open", "commit": "f217b11", "text": "auto-open done (M1-M4). For integration and the first --auto run in CORES: (1) the first press (Reports) comes from a timer 250 ms after captureCores({auto: true}) returns, so the Trace armed by the same paste sees it (self-test auto_then_trace runs that exact order). (2) Auto is proved on the fake page only; CORES has to show whether the script finds the Reports button by its jQuery handler, whether the browser lets CORES open the report window after a script's clicks (if blocked the HUD says so; by hand works), and whether CORES asks something after Reports. (3) Finding 6: a direct-mode capture in a fresh session has no teams or clinical data (nothing pressed Reports) and now ends yellow, not green. (4) Armed mode now says when the browser blocks CORES's report window; new log events auto, auto_handed_over, report_address (cores_log.py unchanged). (5) The HUD self-test now takes about 90 s (31 tests, 365 checks); its runner ignored a test name given first (fixed); --capture FILE now uses auto. (6) Not done: the HUD still shows the report's address in 'CORES opened a report window (...)' (review finding 17)."}
